steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787589615
      nanos: 420098321
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Build a small Python Flask API with one endpoint: POST /predict-urgency\n\nIt should take a JSON body: { \"text\": \"complaint description here\" }\n\nUse scikit-learn to build a simple text classifier:\n- Use TF-IDF vectorization on the input text\n- Train a Logistic Regression model to classify text as \"urgent\" or \"normal\"\n- Since I don\'t have a real dataset yet, generate a synthetic training dataset \n  of about 300 example complaint texts: half labeled \"urgent\" (containing words/\n  phrases suggesting safety risk, harassment, distress, danger, immediate threat) \n  and half labeled \"normal\" (routine complaints like broken fan, slow wifi, food quality)\n- Return a JSON response: { \"urgency_score\": 0.0 to 1.0, \"label\": \"urgent\" or \"normal\" }\n- Include a simple script to evaluate accuracy on a held-out test split and print \n  a confusion matrix, so I can include this in my project report\n\nAlso show me how to save the trained model (pickle or joblib) so it doesn\'t \nretrain every time the server restarts.\n```"
    items {
      text: "Build a small Python Flask API with one endpoint: POST /predict-urgency\n\nIt should take a JSON body: { \"text\": \"complaint description here\" }\n\nUse scikit-learn to build a simple text classifier:\n- Use TF-IDF vectorization on the input text\n- Train a Logistic Regression model to classify text as \"urgent\" or \"normal\"\n- Since I don\'t have a real dataset yet, generate a synthetic training dataset \n  of about 300 example complaint texts: half labeled \"urgent\" (containing words/\n  phrases suggesting safety risk, harassment, distress, danger, immediate threat) \n  and half labeled \"normal\" (routine complaints like broken fan, slow wifi, food quality)\n- Return a JSON response: { \"urgency_score\": 0.0 to 1.0, \"label\": \"urgent\" or \"normal\" }\n- Include a simple script to evaluate accuracy on a held-out test split and print \n  a confusion matrix, so I can include this in my project report\n\nAlso show me how to save the trained model (pickle or joblib) so it doesn\'t \nretrain every time the server restarts.\n```"
    }
  }
}
