  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Crafting the Landing Page**\n\nOkay, I\'m starting to visualize the `src/components/LandingPage.tsx` file. I\'m focusing on that brutalist aesthetic, aiming for a calm and structured feel. First up is the Hero Section - I need to nail that statement of SAGE. Accessibility is key, and I want to ensure the structure is clear and reassuring for the user.\n\n\n**Defining the Hero Section**\n\nI\'ve got the Hero section now. I\'m thinking of \"The Safe, Anonymous Voice for Campus Accountability\" as the core message. I\'ve broken down the subtitle to explain SAGE plainly. There are also dual CTA buttons: \"Submit a Complaint,\" the main one, and \"Browse Complaints\" as a secondary. I\'ve started thinking about the trust badges too, like Zero Account Required.\n\n\n**Analyzing Key Features**\n\nNow, I\'m diving deep into the core features: the dual-signoff on reports for legitimacy, the public audit ledger for transparency, and the anti-retaliation guarantee. I am really understanding the value proposition. The comparison to the physical suggestion box is also becoming clearer, I can really emphasize SAGE\'s advantage with a visual comparison table. Tracking, prioritization, and communication are where SAGE dramatically shines.\n\n\n**Detailing the Anti-Retaliation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the safety aspects and how SAGE ensures anti-retaliation. I\'ve contrasted it with traditional suggestion boxes, highlighting the cryptographic blind submission compared to the risks of interception or recognition. I\'ve started building the 3-step lifecycle for users, focusing on drafting a grievance, the endorsement/escalation process, and official resolution with administrative updates and closure notes.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 19
      nanos: 295655691
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
